{:edb/db-before-t 1635 :edb/db-after-t 1636 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534948 50 #inst "2026-10-08T11:47:22.692Z" 13194139534948 true] [17592186078261 1100 "A loaf of bread just taken out of the oven has a temperature of $180\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}$. After placing it on the table, the temperature $T(t)$ of the bread decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius, and $t > 0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is $25\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}$, which differential equation could be used to model the bread's temperature? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality." 13194139534948 false] [17592186078261 1100 "A loaf of bread just taken out of the oven has a temperature of $180^{\\circ}\\textrm{C}.$ After placing it on the table, the temperature $T(t)$ of the bread decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius, and $t > 0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is $25^{\\circ}\\textrm{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the bread's temperature? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality." 13194139534948 true] [17592186078261 1145 "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = -k(T - T_S)\n$$\n\nwhere $T_S$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nT_S = 25\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = -k(T - 25)\n$$" 13194139534948 false] [17592186078261 1145 "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = -k(T - T_S),\n$$\n\nwhere $T_S$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $T_S = 25^{\\circ}\\textrm{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = -k(T - 25).\n$$" 13194139534948 true] [17592186078262 1100 "The temperature $T(t)$, in degrees Celsius, of a fluorescent light bulb after turning off can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}T}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -0.25(T-23)$\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the bulb is $80\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}$, calculate the temperature of the bulb after $4$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." 13194139534948 false] [17592186078262 1100 "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a fluorescent light bulb after turning off can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}T}{\\mathrm{d}t} =  -0.25(T-23)\n$$\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the bulb is $80^\\circ \\rm C,$ calculate the temperature of the bulb after $4$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." 13194139534948 true] [17592186078262 1145 "We're told that the temperature of the fluorescent light bulb is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}T}{\\mathrm{d}t} =  -0.25(T-23).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-23} & =-0.25\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-23} & =-\\int 0.25\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-23| & =-0.25t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T - 23 > 0$ for all $t > 0.$ \nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-23 & ={e}^{-0.25t+C} \\\\ T-23 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.25t} \\\\ T-23 & =K{e}^{-0.25t} \\\\ T & =23+K{e}^{-0.25t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the light bulb was $80^\\circ \\rm C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=80,$ and solve for $K{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}80 & =23+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 80 & =23+K \\\\ K & =57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t) = 23+57e^{-0.25t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the bulb after $t=4$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(4) = 23+57e^{-0.25\\cdot 4}\\approx 44^\\circ\\textrm{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." 13194139534948 true] [17592186078262 1145 "We're told that the temperature of the fluorescent light bulb is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}T}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -0.25(T-23)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dT}{T - 23} &= -0.25dt \\\\\n\\int \\frac{dT}{T - 23} &= - \\int 0.25dt \\\\\n\\ln \\mid T - 23 \\mid &= -0.25t + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T - 23 > 0$ for all $t > 0$.\nNext, we solve for $T(t)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT - 23 &= e^{-0.25t + C} \\\\\nT - 23 &= e^{C}e^{-0.25t} \\\\\nT - 23 &= Ke^{-0.25t} \\\\\nT &= 23 + Ke^{-0.25t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the light bulb was $80\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}$. So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=80$, and solve for $K{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n80 &= 23 + Ke^{0} \\\\\n80 &= 23 + K \\\\\nK &= 57\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t) = 23+57e^{-0.25t}\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the bulb after $t=4$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(4) = 23+57e^{-0.25\\cdot 4}\\approx 44\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." 13194139534948 false] [17592186078295 1100 "Given that $y > 50$ and $t > 0$, which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600+12y$\n2. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y$\n3. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y^2$" 13194139534948 false] [17592186078295 1100 "Given that $y > 50$ and $t > 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600+12y \\[5pt]$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y \\[5pt]$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y^2$" 13194139534948 true] [17592186078295 1145 "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = -k(y - a)\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y > a$ for all $t > 0$.\n\nFrom the given options, 2 is the only inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = -12(y-50)\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=12$, and $a=50$. Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y > 50$ for all $t > 0$." 13194139534948 false] [17592186078295 1145 "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = -k(y - a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y > a$ for all $t > 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, II is the only inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = -12(y-50).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=12,$ and $a=50.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y > 50$ for all $t > 0.$" 13194139534948 true] [17592186078499 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321777 13194139534948 true] [17592186078499 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321784 13194139534948 true] [17592186078499 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321791 13194139534948 true] [17592186078500 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321798 13194139534948 true] [17592186078500 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321805 13194139534948 true] [17592186078500 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321812 13194139534948 true] [17592186078500 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321819 13194139534948 true] [17592186078501 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321826 13194139534948 true] [17592186078501 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321833 13194139534948 true] [17592186078501 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321840 13194139534948 true] [17592186078501 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321847 13194139534948 true] [17592186078501 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321854 13194139534948 true] [17592186078623 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534948 false] [17592186078623 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534948 true] [17592186078623 1145 "An inhibited decay initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2(y-500),\\,y(0)=600.\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=2,a=500,$ and ${y}_{0}=600.$ Notice that ${y}_{0}>a,$ which is required for inhibited decay. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited decay. In particular, the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2(y-500),\\,y(0)=400,\n$$\n\ndoes *not* represent inhibited decay model because ${y}_{0}=400<a=500.$" 13194139534948 true] [17592186078623 1145 "Inhibited decay has the form $y^{\\prime}=-k(y-a)$ with $k>0$ and $y(0)>a$. The equation $y^{\\prime}=-2(y-500)$ has equilibrium $a=500$, and $y(0)=600>500$. Indeed, its solution is $y(t)=500+100e^{-2t}$, which decreases toward $500$." 13194139534948 false] [17592186078625 1100 "Given that $y > 20$ and $t > 0$, which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" 13194139534948 false] [17592186078625 1100 "Given that $y>20$ and $t>0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" 13194139534948 true] [17592186078625 1145 "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-80(y-20).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=80,$ and $a=20.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y>20$ for all $t>0.$" 13194139534948 true] [17592186078625 1145 "The inhibited decay form is $y^{\\prime}=-k(y-a)$ with $k>0$ and $y>a$. Here $-80(y-20)$ has $k=80$ and $a=20$. For $y>20$ the derivative is negative, so the solution decreases toward $20$." 13194139534948 false] [17592186078798 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534948 false] [17592186078798 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534948 true] [17592186078798 1100 "A medical doctor records the temperature of $100\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{F}$ using a thermometer. After the measurement is taken, the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t > 0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is $64\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{F}$, which differential equation could be used to model the thermometer reading? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality." 13194139534948 false] [17592186078798 1100 "A medical doctor records the temperature of ${100}^{∘}\\text{F}$ using a thermometer. After the measurement is taken, the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is ${64}^{∘}\\text{F},$ which differential equation could be used to model the thermometer reading? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." 13194139534948 true] [17592186078798 1145 "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={64}^{∘}\\text{F},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-64).\n$$" 13194139534948 true] [17592186078798 1145 "Newton's law of cooling uses the difference between the thermometer temperature and the room temperature: $T-64$. Therefore $\\frac{dT}{dt}=-k(T-64)$ with $k>0$. The initial reading gives $T(0)=100$; it does not replace the ambient temperature in the differential equation." 13194139534948 false] [17592186321777 1017 #edb/ref 17592186321778 13194139534948 true] [17592186321777 1089 "q-106647" 13194139534948 true] [17592186321777 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534948 true] [17592186321777 1100 "Given that $y>9$ and $t>0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" 13194139534948 true] [17592186321777 1113 #uuid "4ab835de-6077-5001-a0a7-4516bf0305aa" 13194139534948 true] [17592186321777 1145 "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5(y-9).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=5,$ and $a=9.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y>9$ for all $t>0.$" 13194139534948 true] [17592186321778 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321779 13194139534948 true] [17592186321778 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321780 13194139534948 true] [17592186321778 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321781 13194139534948 true] [17592186321778 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321782 13194139534948 true] [17592186321778 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321783 13194139534948 true] [17592186321778 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534948 true] [17592186321778 1041 "selection" 13194139534948 true] [17592186321778 1104 #edb/ref 17592186321780 13194139534948 true] [17592186321778 1110 #uuid "894a2987-4984-5afc-a234-5be706772146" 13194139534948 true] [175921863